    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1193">

                <pb facs="0975" n="859" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg859"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/5315.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Consul at Vera Cruz to the Secretary of
                        State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Vera Cruz</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-10-22T10:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 22, 1912—10 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Foreigners and noncombatants are between consulate and the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Des Moines</hi>, which lies 2,000 feet away. This zone
                    has been designated by myself and colleagues as neutral. Commanders on both
                    sides promise to respect and to protect it, being convinced that if they do not
                    Commander Hughes will, even if he has to land armed forces. I have taken all
                    possible steps to protect life and properties, even established hospital in free
                    zone, and have mounted messengers to be able to act quickly when battle
                    commences at 12 o’clock a.m., as promised.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Canada.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
